{"findings":[{"claim_id":"c1,c2,c3","quote":"Zelvoran® (zelvoran) is indicated for the treatment of moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis in adults who are candidates for systemic therapy or phototherapy.

In a 16-week randomised, placebo-controlled trial, 68.4% of adults treated with Zelvoran achieved PASI-75 compared with 9.2% receiving placebo [1].

92% of adults maintained completely clear skin at two years.","issue":"Four benefit claims are presented before any risk information appears, violating the fair_balance threshold for unqualified benefit claims.","explanation":"The piece opens with three efficacy claims back-to-back—the indication statement, the 68.4% PASI-75 result, and the 92% two-year skin-clearance claim—before the reader encounters any safety information. The validator measured 4 unqualified benefit claims appearing before the first risk mention, against a threshold of 2. This sequence lets readers absorb substantial benefit messaging without any counterbalance, even though the safety section exists later in the piece.","suggested_fix":"Move the Important Safety Information section to immediately after the indication statement (claim c1), before the efficacy results. This ensures readers encounter key contraindications and warnings as they consider whether the drug is appropriate, rather than only after a series of benefit claims.","severity":"advisory","concern":"fair_balance","tags":[],"confidence":95,"citations":[{"source_id":"fair_balance_validator","passage":"unqualified_benefit_claims: claims_before_first_risk: 4, threshold: 2, verdict: fail"}]},{"claim_id":"","quote":"Zelvoran is contraindicated in active, serious infection, including active tuberculosis. Serious infections, hepatic enzyme elevations and serious hypersensitivity reactions have been reported. The effect of Zelvoran on the risk of malignancy is not known. The most common adverse reactions were nasopharyngitis (14.1%), injection-site reaction (9.4%) and headache (7.2%).","issue":"The safety section does not convey the requirement to screen for latent tuberculosis and consider anti-tuberculosis therapy before starting ZELVORAN, nor the instruction to monitor liver enzymes and avoid live vaccines.","explanation":"The piece mentions tuberculosis only as a contraindication (active disease), but the label requires disclosure that patients must be screened for latent tuberculosis and may need preventive therapy. It mentions hepatic enzyme elevations occurred but omits the directive to monitor liver enzymes at baseline and periodically. The piece also fails to state that live vaccines should be avoided during treatment. These are actionable precautions that clinicians and patients need to know; their omission leaves the safety profile incomplete.","suggested_fix":"Add to the safety section: 'Screen for latent tuberculosis before initiating treatment and consider anti-tuberculosis therapy if latent infection is found. Monitor liver enzymes at baseline and periodically during treatment. Avoid live vaccines during treatment.'","severity":"advisory","concern":"safety_completeness","tags":[],"confidence":90,"citations":[{"source_id":"acme_pharma:label:zelvoran:v1.0:4","passage":"Tuberculosis. Screen for latent tuberculosis before initiating treatment and consider anti-tuberculosis therapy before starting ZELVORAN in patients with latent infection."},{"source_id":"acme_pharma:label:zelvoran:v1.0:4","passage":"Hepatic enzyme elevations. Elevations in ALT or AST greater than three times the upper limit of normal occurred in 2.1% of ZELVORAN-treated patients compared with 0.6% receiving placebo. Monitor liver enzymes at baseline and periodically during treatment."},{"source_id":"acme_pharma:label:zelvoran:v1.0:4","passage":"Immunisations. Avoid use of live vaccines during treatment."}]}]}
